          "Claude 3.5 Haiku": "Here are several approaches to calculate π (pi) in JavaScript:\n\n1. Leibniz Formula (Basic Method):\n```javascript\nfunction calculatePi(iterations) {\n    let pi = 0;\n    for (let i = 0; i < iterations; i++) {\n        pi += Math.pow(-1, i) / (2 * i + 1);\n    }\n    return 4 * pi;\n}\n\nconsole.log(calculatePi(1000000)); // More iterations = more precision\n```\n\n2. Monte Carlo Simulation:\n```javascript\nfunction calculatePiMonteCarlo(totalPoints) {\n    let insideCircle = 0;\n    \n    for (let i = 0; i < totalPoints; i++) {\n        const x = Math.random();\n        const y = Math.random();\n        \n        if (x*x + y*y <= 1) {\n            insideCircle++;\n        }\n    }\n    \n    return 4 * (insideCircle / totalPoints);\n}\n\nconsole.log(calculatePiMonteCarlo(1000000));\n```\n\n3. Nilakantha Series:\n```javascript\nfunction calculatePiNilakantha(iterations) {\n    let pi = 3;\n    let sign = 1;\n    \n    for (let i = 2; i < iterations * 2; i += 2) {\n        pi += sign * (4 / (i * (i + 1) * (i + 2)));\n        sign *= -1;\n    }\n    \n    return pi;\n}\n\nconsole.log(calculatePiNilakantha(1000));\n```\n\n4. Buffon's Needle Method:\n```javascript\nfunction calculatePiBuffon(needles) {\n    let crossedNeedles = 0;\n    \n    for (let i = 0; i < needles; i++) {\n        const angle = Math.random() * Math.PI / 2;\n        const distance = Math.random();\n        \n        if (distance <= Math.sin(angle)) {\n            crossedNeedles++;\n        }\n    }\n    \n    return (2 * needles) / crossedNeedles;\n}\n\nconsole.log(calculatePiBuffon(100000));\n```\n\n5. Built-in Math.PI (Most Accurate):\n```javascript\nconsole.log(Math.PI); // JavaScript's built-in value\n```\n\nEach method has pros and cons:\n- Leibniz Formula: Simple but converges slowly\n- Monte Carlo: Probabilistic approximation\n- Nilakantha: Faster convergence\n- Buffon's Needle: Interesting geometric approach\n- Math.PI: Most precise, built-in constant\n\nChoose based on your specific requirements and precision needs.\n\nBonus Tip: For high-precision calculations, consider using libraries like decimal.js or big.js.\n\nExample with Error Calculation:\n```javascript\nfunction testPiMethod(method, iterations) {\n    const calculated = method(iterations);\n    const actual = Math.PI;\n    const error = Math.abs(calculated - actual);\n    \n    console.log(`Calculated: ${calculated}`);\n    console.log(`Actual: ${actual}`);\n    console.log(`Error: ${error}`);\n}\n\ntestPiMethod(calculatePi, 1000000);\n```\n\nEach method will give slightly different results, so experiment to find what works best for your use case!",
