        "rejected": "\n\nHuman: I like to host guests at my home from time to time, and I am gathering  recipes of different dishes and drinks to keep things interesting. I am interested in trying some  German dishes. Can you give me a recipe for Zigeunerschnitzel?\n\nAssistant: Sure! Here’s the recipe:\n\nZigeunerschnitzel (aka zigeuner mit knödeln)\n\n1 lb. pork loin\n\n1 cup flour\n\n1/2 cup milk\n\n1/2 cup cracker crumbs\n\n2 eggs, beaten\n\n6 oz. grated parmesan\n\nSalt and pepper to taste\n\nButter for frying\n\nFlour for dredging\n\nPeanut oil\n\nInstructions:\n\nCut the pork loin in half lengthwise.  Remove any gristle and fat from inside.  Use a meat tenderizer or mallet and pound into thin meat slices (think churrasco, but you’ll be tenderizing).  Mix flour and a little milk into a sticky batter.  Put a thin layer of flour in a bowl and set aside.  Lay the pork slices out on the flour in a single layer and dip each slice into the batter, coating both sides.  Fry until golden brown on both sides, about 2 minutes per side.  Set aside. \n\nTake about 1 cup of the cooked pork slices and mince them very fine (think your mom’s meatloaf, or sauerkraut.  Put the rest of the pork into a bowl and add the mince, cracker crumbs, Parmesan, and eggs.  Make sure the crackers are completely blended in with the rest of the ingredients.  Add some salt and pepper to taste.  Divide into small, flat patties and place on a baking sheet.  Cover with foil and place in a warm place.  Preheat the oven to 350 F. \n\nIn a skillet, melt about a tablespoon of butter.  Place the patties in the skillet and place in the oven for about 5 minutes.  Turn over.  Cover.  Let cook another 5 minutes.  Take out and set on a plate and repeat with the rest of the patties.  The patties should be browned on the outside and done all the way through, just slightly pink in the center."
